8:       // Topic filter (multi-topic matching!)\r\n1799:       if (activeTopic && activeTopic !== 'all') {\r\n1800:         list = list.filter(p => (p.topics && p.topics.includes(activeTopic)) || p.topic === activeTopic);\r\n1801:  \n<truncated 202 bytes>\ntern);\r\n1806:       }\r\n1807: \r\n1808:       // Difficulty filter\r\n1809:       if (this.selectedDifficulty && this.selectedDifficulty !== 'all') {\r\n1810:         list = list.filter(p => p.difficulty === this.selectedDifficulty);\r\n1811:       }\r\n1812: \r\n1813:       // Status filter\r\n1814:       if (this.selectedStatus && this.selectedStatus !== 'all') {\r\n1815:         if (this.selectedStatus === 'solved') {\r\n1816:           list = list.filter(p => this.progress.solved.includes(p.id));\r\n1817:         } else if (this.selectedStatus === 'attempted') {\r\n1818:           list = list.filter(p => this.progress.attempted.includes(p.id) && !this.progress.solved.includes(p.id));\r\n1819:         } else if (this.selectedStatus === 'not_started') {\r\n1820:           list = list.filter(p => !this.progress.solved.includes(p.id) && !this.progress.attempted.includes(p.id));\r\n1821:         } else if (this.selectedStatus === 'bookmarked') {\r\n1822:           list = list.filter(p => this.progress.bookmarked.includes(p.id));\r\n1823:         } else if (this.selectedStatus === 'with_notes') {\r\n1824:           list = list.filter(p => this.hasNote(p.id));\r\n1825:         }\r\n1826:       }\r\n1827: \r\n1828:       // Sorting\r\n1829:       const diffRank = { Easy: 1, Medium: 2, Hard: 3 };\r\n1830:       if (this.selectedSort === 'lc_asc') {\r\n1831:         list.sort((a, b) => a.leetcodeNumber - b.leetcodeNumber);\r\n1832:       } else if (this.selectedSort === 'lc_desc') {\r\n1833:         list.sort((a, b) => b.leetcodeNumber - a.leetcodeNumber);\r\n1834:       } else if (this.selectedSort === 'diff_asc') {\r\n1835:         list.sort((a, b) => (diffRank[a.difficulty] || 0) - (diffRank[b.difficulty] || 0));\r\n1836:       } else if (this.selectedSort === 'title_asc') {\r\n1837:         list.sort((a, b) => a.title.localeCompare(b.title));\r\n1838:       }\r\n1839: \r\n1840:       return list;\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":2098,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:54:55Z","input_tokens":2151,"cache_read_tokens":108899,"output_tokens":8420,"thinking":"Initial focus is on refining the problem filtering mechanism. The current approach will be extended to incorporate a full-text search capability. This will allow for matching against multiple fields within each problem description, including title, LeetCode number, pattern, description, summary, and topic. The goal is to provide more flexible and comprehensive search functionality.\n\n\nImplemented full-text search across multiple problem description fields: title, LeetCode number, pattern, description, summary, and topic. The updated filtering logic combines full-text search with existing filters for topic, pattern, difficulty, and status. Current work focuses on refining the status filter to accurately reflect problem progress.\n\n\nRefining the status filter necessitates defining logic for \"attempted,\" \"not_started,\" \"review,\" \"bookmarked,\" and \"with_notes\" states, ensuring these are mutually exclusive where appropriate. Independently applied bookmark filter ensures bookmarked problems are always visible, regardless of other status filters.\n\nImplementing sorting options by title (ascending/descending), difficulty (ascending/descending), and learning order (ascending/descending) is the next step to enhance usability. The difficulty sort will use a rank assigned to each difficulty level to facilitate comparison.\n\n\nSorting logic is being implemented for the problem list. Sorting will include ascending and descending options for title, difficulty (using pre-defined difficulty ranks), and LeetCode number or ID as a fallback. Pagination logic is extracted to calculate the total number of pages and adjust the current page if it exceeds the calculated number of pages. Finally, the table metadata bar's content will be updated to accurately reflect the filtered and paginated problem data, including the total filtered count, the range of problems being displayed, and the total number of problems.\n\n\nThe table metadata bar will be updated to accurately display filtered problem counts, the displayed problem range, and the total n\n<truncated 125 bytes>\non logic incorporates ellipsis for condensed page number representation.\n\n\nPagination controls are being constructed to enable navigation through problem sets. Disabled states for navigation buttons are being implemented based on current page and total pages. Ellipsis is incorporated into page number display for condensed representation. The table metadata bar will dynamically reflect the filtered and paginated problem data.\n\n\nThe code suggests adding visual indicators for bookmarks, review status, and saved notes within the problem list. The presence of a \"Review\" badge, note indicator, or bookmark star will now signal the problem's status. The problem modal's route will be cleaned up on modal close.\n\n\nDirect routes to individual problems are being implemented using URL fragments to enable easy sharing. The modal will now include a route bar with a direct link and a copy button.\n\nBookmarking, review status, and solved state are integrated into the modal footer, including actions for toggling each state. The problem's route will be removed upon modal closure to maintain a clean URL. A copy route button and corresponding feedback mechanism are also incorporated.\n\n\nModal functionality is being added to allow for directly sharing problem routes via a URL fragment, including a copy-to-clipboard button with feedback. Review state toggle is integrated, and the route is cleaned up on modal close. The import modal is being implemented with appropriate header elements, including a title and close button. The modal's backdrop and card styling are being defined, with the intention of adding import functionality later.\n\n\nThe import modal requires parsing a JSON array of pro